ittee hearings mark warner the  senator spoke about a misinformation  campaign spreading fake news and up to  fit I think several key states during  the election we're talking about 15,000  operatives who may have been involved in  a broader campaign of misinformation do  you think the public is aware enough  about what may have happened and that  there is a sufficient amount of alarm in  terms of these investigations well I  think the Senate investigation is very  serious there they're working together  at this point they're making that  bipartisan I don't think there's enough  awareness of this this this is a big  deal but it's it's a big deal from the  point of view of how do you stop it how  do you get it out in the open so we can  have free elections where the process is  untampered with by the russians or  others Bob what do you think Glenn might  have to say about this that might  contribute to our understanding well he  key player I know Flynn a little bit  talked to him in January and he insists  that the whole approach by President  Trump in Russia is kind of a two-track  strategy reach out to Putin but also  doing some very tough things with our  military that Putin is going to hate so  the real policy here is yet to emerge  that's that's part of this story I mean  the difficulty here is there is a  context of a lot of hysteria a lot of  overreaching I think on both sides and  as the old former editor  The Washington Post Ben Bradlee used to  say slow down  the truth emerges in the Internet age  with all the tweets and so forth that's  very difficult well talking about the  truth emerging in Nora's interview with  Paul Ryan he was a term whistleblower as  a to describe the source involved what  do you make of the use of that word in  this circumstance given what you know  yeah I don't think it's the case this is  somebody on the National Security  Council staff my paper is reported and  confirmed and expanded on what the New  York Times said here it's a very strange  idea as I said that you would get an  information from the White House and  then have to go down and brief the  president not the president presumably  can call anyone on his staff in and say  what's going on what does the  administration have a narrative to tell  based on these recent things that  chairman no Nunez talked about to  explain the president's tweets I I  haven't seen it and that that's of  course what's gonna happen if you go  back to fumbled investigations like in  water gave where the White House and  President Nixon were covering up some  other times I think the White House and  other administration's has said oh  there's this investigation it's serious  business let's do our own inquiry and  get the facts out that's what President  Trump should do here unless the facts  are going to be extremely damaging but  transparency is always the answer get  out ahead of it whether that's going to  happen we will see all right Bob  Woodward thank you very much well to  leave it there I'm sure we'll talk to  you another time  thank you sir Thanks
 

